    {
      "id": "metabolism",
      "heading": "Absorption, Metabolism and Losses",
      "bullets": [
        "Fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) need bile and dietary fat for absorption; stored in liver/adipose; toxicity possible (A, D). Water-soluble (B, C) are not stored (except B12 in liver) and are excreted.",
        "Vitamin D is synthesised in skin by UVB and activated by liver and kidney to calcitriol.",
        "Vitamin B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor from the stomach.",
        "Losses: vitamin C (heat, oxidation, leaching), thiamine (heat, alkali, sulphite), riboflavin (light), folate (heat); retain by steaming, minimal water, covering containers.",
        "Avidin in raw egg white binds biotin; polished-rice diets cause thiamine deficiency."
      ]
    }
  ],
